# 12. Regulatory and Mine-Safety Context

This is not an artificial college problem.

Indian mine-safety regulations and DGMS safety communications have repeatedly addressed risks involving dumpers, tippers, trucks and other transportation machinery in opencast mines.

## DGMS Evidence

A DGMS circular issued in August 2024 specifically addressed safety in opencast coal mines and the prevention of accidents involving wheeled trackless transportation machinery such as dumpers and tippers.

DGMS reported that, in its analysis of fatal accidents during 2023, **36% were caused by dumpers, tippers, trucks and similar vehicles**. It further identified run-overs, being hit by dumpers, head-on collisions and other vehicle-related incidents among the causes. ([DGMS][2])

DGMS also issued a **2025 safety alert** concerning a fatal head-to-tail collision between two tippers in an opencast coal mine. ([DGMS][3])

The DGMS safety-alert records for 2025 additionally include multiple fatal incidents involving trucks, tippers, dumpers and related heavy machinery in open-cast mining operations. ([DGMS][4])

Therefore:

> **Heavy-vehicle interaction + visibility + haul-road safety is a real mine-safety engineering problem.**

